From: Chris Blake <chrisrblake93@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:34:48 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] [LOCAL] mb/radxa/x4: Add a UART flash mode and Top Swap
 recovery

With RADXA_X4_UART_FLASH, the bootblock waits 2 s for "x4flash" on
UART0. If it arrives, it serves a CRC32-framed protocol to
scripts/x4_uart_flash.py, which rewrites only the 4 KiB blocks that
differ from a new image. A build that fails after the bootblock can
then be reflashed without an SPI programmer.

RADXA_X4_RECOVERY builds a second bootblock for the Top Swap block
(0xf80000-0xfbffff). It serves the same protocol until Top Swap is
cleared and never boots on. Its FIT and microcode sit in its top
256KB, so the cut-out block is complete when the PCH maps it to the
top of the 4GB window. Other builds take that block as
RADXA_X4_RECOVERY_BOOTBLOCK_FILE.

- It runs in the bootblock because FSP-M is located and verified
  before any romstage hook runs.
- Writes are limited to hard-coded ranges, checked against the FMAP
  at build time. HELLO lists every flash range with its name and what
  the role does with it, so the host tool carries no layout of its
  own. Neither role writes the descriptor, the ME or the block it runs
  from. The normal role writes RW_MRC_CACHE, SMMSTORE and COREBOOT
  below the Top Swap block, and the Top Swap block after an explicit
  unlock. The recovery role also writes descriptor region 9, and the
  FMAP and the top 256KB after an explicit unlock.
- The normal role can set BUC.TS and the recovery role can clear it.
  Changing the bit swaps the block the code runs from at once, so a
  34-byte stub copied into CAR changes it and resets. The normal role
  sets the bit only when the Top Swap block holds a reset vector and a
  FIT. The recovery role clears it only when the normal top block
  holds them and the FMAP is the one the normal bootblock checks for.
- The recovery role clears BUC.TS after 10 minutes without a valid
  frame, and on die(), unless it wrote flash in this boot. With the
  Top Swap strap set the bit stays set, and it keeps serving.
- The recovery role checks that reads by flash offset don't follow
  Top Swap by comparing the FIT pointer read both ways. If they do, it
  refuses to unlock the top block, keeps Top Swap set and says so in
  HELLO.
- If the FMAP doesn't match the layout the normal bootblock was built
  for, the normal role still answers HELLO, CRCS, READ, RESET and
  TOPSWAP 1, so the board can enter recovery, but refuses WRITE and
  UNLOCK.
- Timeouts read the TSC: timer_monotonic_get() drops the
  sub-microsecond remainder on every call, which makes these polling
  loops run about a quarter too long.
- Identical blocks are skipped. Others are erased, written and read
  back, with one retry.
- Before the first erase the bootblock reads all of its own code into
  the cache, because it runs from the flash chip it erases.
- After any write, EXIT resets instead of booting on.

